= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At <large-deviation speed> $L$, the <exponential distribution> gives the <large deviation principle> on $\mathbb R$ with <good rate function>
$$
\boxed{J(b)=\begin{cases}\lambda b,&b\geq0,\\+\infty,&b<0.\end{cases}}
$$
In particular, $\mathbb P(B/L\geq b)=e^{-\lambda Lb}$ for $b\geq0$. Its finite <sublevel sets> are the <compact sets> $[0,r/\lambda]$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $S_L=\sum_{i=1}^LA_i$ and $Z_L=S_L/L$. The <cumulant-generating function> of one <normal random variable> is
$$
\kappa(\theta)=\log\mathbb E e^{\theta A_1}=\mu\theta+\frac{\sigma^2\theta^2}{2},\qquad \theta\in\mathbb R.
$$
<Cramér's theorem> states that empirical means of <independent and identically distributed random variables> whose <moment-generating function> is finite on a neighborhood of zero satisfy a <large deviation principle> at the sample-size <large-deviation speed>, with <good rate function> equal to the <Legendre-Fenchel transform> of their <cumulant-generating function>. Here that gives
$$
I(x)=\sup_{\theta\in\mathbb R}\left\{\theta(x-\mu)-\frac{\sigma^2\theta^2}{2}\right\}.
$$
For $\sigma^2>0$, differentiation gives the maximizing parameter $\theta=(x-\mu)/\sigma^2$, and hence
$$
\boxed{I(x)=\frac{(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}.}
$$
This <rate function> tends to infinity as $|x|\to\infty$, so its finite <sublevel sets> are <compact>. Equivalently, the <Gärtner–Ellis theorem> applies because the limiting scaled <cumulant-generating function> is finite and differentiable everywhere; it is <lower semicontinuous> and <essentially smooth>, with no finite domain boundary at which to check steepness. If $\sigma^2=0$ is permitted, $Z_L=\mu$ deterministically and the <rate function> is zero at $\mu$ and infinity elsewhere.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <product large-deviation principle> states that independent families satisfying <large deviation principles> at the same speed with <good rate functions> $J$ and $I$ have joint <good rate function> $J(b)+I(z)$. The <contraction principle for large deviations> states that a <continuous map> $f$ sends such a principle to one with <good rate function> $\inf_{f(u)=x}I(u)$. Apply these results to $(B/L,S_L/L)$ and addition. The resulting <infimal convolution> is
$$
K(x)=\inf_{b\geq0}\left\{\lambda b+\frac{(x-b-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}\right\}.
$$
For $\sigma^2>0$, the unconstrained stationary point is $b=x-\mu-\lambda\sigma^2$. <Convexity> gives the constrained minimum at $b_*=(x-\mu-\lambda\sigma^2)^+$, so
$$
\boxed{K(x)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2},&x\leq\mu+\lambda\sigma^2,\\\lambda(x-\mu)-\dfrac{\lambda^2\sigma^2}{2},&x\geq\mu+\lambda\sigma^2.\end{cases}}
$$
The two branches agree in value and first derivative at their junction. Both tails tend to infinity, giving a <good rate function>. This is the <fixed exponential perturbation of a Gaussian empirical mean>.

There is a reason to use the <product large-deviation principle> rather than invoke the supplied <Gärtner–Ellis theorem> without checking its hypotheses. The limiting scaled <cumulant-generating function> here is
$$
\Lambda(\theta)=\begin{cases}\mu\theta+\sigma^2\theta^2/2,&\theta<\lambda,\\+\infty,&\theta\geq\lambda.\end{cases}
$$
At $\lambda$ it is neither <lower semicontinuous> nor steep from the left: its left derivative tends to the finite value $\mu+\lambda\sigma^2$. Its <Legendre-Fenchel transform> still equals $K$, but the stated <Gärtner–Ellis theorem> does not establish the full lower bound on the linear branch. The <product large-deviation principle> and <contraction principle for large deviations> do establish it. In the degenerate case $\sigma^2=0$, the answer is $K(x)=\lambda(x-\mu)$ for $x\geq\mu$, and infinity otherwise.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The sum still has a <normal distribution>, now with
$$
\mathbb E\frac{C+S_L}{L}=\mu+\frac{\nu}{L},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}\frac{C+S_L}{L}=\frac{\sigma^2}{L}+\frac{\rho^2}{L^2}.
$$
Its scaled <cumulant-generating function> is
$$
\frac1L\log\mathbb E e^{\theta(C+S_L)}
=\mu\theta+\frac{\sigma^2\theta^2}{2}+\frac{\nu\theta+\rho^2\theta^2/2}{L}.
$$
The limit is the same everywhere-finite function as in part (b), so the <Gärtner–Ellis theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{I_C(x)=\frac{(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}}
$$
at <large-deviation speed> $L$, when $\sigma^2>0$.

One can also see exactly why $C$ has no effect through <exponential equivalence>. For every $\varepsilon>0$, the <Chernoff bound> for the two <Gaussian tail bounds> gives, when $L\varepsilon>|\nu|$ and $\rho^2>0$,
$$
\mathbb P(|C|>L\varepsilon)\leq2\exp\left(-\frac{(L\varepsilon-|\nu|)^2}{2\rho^2}\right).
$$
Its logarithm divided by $L$ tends to $-\infty$. If $\rho^2=0$, this probability is eventually zero. The transfer result for <exponential equivalence> says that coupled families whose distance exceeds each fixed positive tolerance with superexponentially small probability share a <large deviation principle> with the same <good rate function>. Apply it to $(C+S_L)/L$ and $S_L/L$. This also covers $\sigma^2=0$, with the point-mass <rate function> from part (b).

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

All three normalized sums converge in probability to $\mu$: the <weak law of large numbers> controls $S_L/L$, and the two fixed <random variables> vanish after division by $L$. Their rare fluctuations nevertheless differ. A fixed <normal random variable> would have to fluctuate by order $L$ to alter the mean by a fixed amount, and that costs $\exp(-\text{constant}\,L^2)$. A fixed <exponential random variable> can do the same for a cost of order $\exp(-\text{constant}\,L)$, exactly the <large-deviation speed> of the empirical mean.

Below $\mu+\lambda\sigma^2$, the optimal exponential contribution is zero, and the <Gaussian> empirical mean supplies the fluctuation. Above it, the optimal empirical mean remains at $\mu+\lambda\sigma^2$, while $B/L$ supplies $x-\mu-\lambda\sigma^2$. This explains the quadratic-to-linear change in the <rate function> and why only the upper tail is altered. **Convergence to the same typical value does not imply the same large-deviation behavior.**

